<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reichsdeputationshauptschluss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Imperial Recess of 1803</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reichsdeputationshauptschluss, or Imperial Recess, was the final resolution of an imperial deputation adopted at Regensburg in 1803. To compensate German princes for lands lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, it secularised almost all church states and abolished most imperial cities, handing their territories to the larger princes. It created new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>, among them Württemberg and Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, and reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> before the Empire ended in 1806.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss.jpg" alt="Reichsdeputationshauptschluss" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Känsterle at nl.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titelseite_des_Reichsdeputationshauptschlusses_vom_25._Februar_1803.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…French in May, and the elective Holy Roman crown was visibly failing after the territorial upheavals of the <b>Imperial Recess of 1803</b>, so Francis took a new hereditary title, Emperor of Austria, as Francis I. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the Habsburgs had…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the <b>Imperial Recess</b> (Reichsdeputationshauptschluss) of 1803 the right-bank lands, including Heidelberg and Mannheim, went mainly to Baden, with smaller parts to Hesse-Darmstadt, the princes of Leiningen and others, while Elector Maximilian IV Joseph was compensated in Franconia and Swabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1803 the <b>Imperial Recess</b> left Bremen among the six free imperial cities that survived the reorganisation of the Empire, and after Napoleonic rule the Congress of Vienna confirmed it as one of the four free cities of the German Confederation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…who succeeded in 1790, lost his small territories west of the Rhine to revolutionary France, and in the <b>Imperial Recess of 1803</b> he was compensated with the Duchy of Westphalia, formerly ruled by the Archbishop-Electors of Cologne, together with lands of the Archbishopric of Mainz and the Electoral…</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Imperial Recess of 1803</b> had already given Bavaria the Franconian prince-bishoprics of Bamberg and Würzburg (the latter given up again in 1805), other church lands and imperial cities, and the Peace of Pressburg in 1805 added Tyrol, Vorarlberg and further Swabian territory together with full sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ruler, Frederick II, Duke of Württemberg from 1797, was a large and forceful prince who had been raised to the dignity of Elector in 1803, when the <b>Imperial Recess</b> compensated German princes for lands lost west of the Rhine with secularised church territories and former imperial cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
